# zenflate checksum SIMD vs scalar on aarch64 (NEON) — first ARM measurement
date:    2026-07-28
host:    Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
build:   release, NO -C target-cpu=native
method:  compile-time A/B on the `simd` cargo feature (this crate gates its SIMD
         behind a feature rather than a runtime token, so the honest comparison
         is two builds, not a token toggle):
           on : cargo bench --bench throughput -- checksum
           off: cargo bench --bench throughput --no-default-features \
                  --features std,alloc,compress,threads -- checksum

## Result

| checksum | SIMD (NEON) | scalar    | speedup |
|----------|-------------|-----------|---------|
| crc32    |   19.48us   | 405.5us   | 20.8x   |
| adler32  |   15.72us   | 190.4us   | 12.1x   |

The largest SIMD payoff measured across the zen crates on this host by a wide
margin (next highest: zenpng 3.6-7.7x, linear-srgb 3.3-6.6x). Expected for
checksums: CRC32 uses carry-less multiply (PMULL, via the `neon_aes` tier) and
Adler32 vectorises cleanly, so the scalar path is genuinely bit-at-a-time work.

Harness sanity check: the libdeflate reference row is unchanged across the two
builds (14.11 -> 14.08us adler32, 11.86 -> 11.80us crc32), so the swing is
zenflate's own code, not drift between runs.

## Open lead — crc32 is still 1.64x behind libdeflate on ARM

Even with NEON on, zenflate crc32 is 19.48us vs libdeflate's 11.86us on the
same input. adler32 is much closer (15.72 vs 14.11us, 1.11x). libdeflate's ARM
CRC32 uses a multi-stream PMULL folding schedule; if CRC throughput matters
here, that gap is the thing to chase. NOT investigated on this pass — recorded
so it is not mistaken for parity.

(crc32fast, the pure-Rust reference, is 84.0us — zenflate's NEON path is 4.3x
faster than it.)
